<p>Copying a key means finding a blank with the right profile for your lock, then
cutting your original's depths onto it. That works for the great majority of
everyday keys. It does not work when the blank is legally controlled, or when
the key carries electronics that need programming rather than grinding.</p>

<h2>What a blank actually is</h2>

<p>A blank is an uncut key shaped for one manufacturer's keyway. Look at the
long edge of your key and you will see a profile of grooves and ridges running
its length. Those are not decorative: they are what lets the key enter that
cylinder at all, before any of the cutting matters. Kwikset and Schlage, the two
most common residential brands in this area, use different profiles and are not
interchangeable.</p>

<p>So a copy is a two-step match. First the right blank, which is about the
brand and keyway. Then the cuts, which are about the depths ground into the
blade. Get the blank wrong and the key will not go in; get the depths wrong and
it goes in but will not turn.</p>

