<h2>The everyday cases</h2>

<p><strong>House and office keys.</strong> Standard pin-tumbler keys on common
keyways. Copy quickly and reliably.</p>

<p><strong>Padlock keys.</strong> Usually straightforward, though some smaller
padlocks use proprietary blanks that are harder to source.</p>

<p><strong>Mailbox and cabinet keys.</strong> Often small flat keys on their own
keyways. Frequently copyable, occasionally not.</p>

<h2>What cannot be copied, and why</h2>

<p><strong>Restricted and patented keyways.</strong> If your key is stamped
"do not duplicate", or came from a managed system in an office building, the
blank itself is controlled. The manufacturer only sells it to authorised
dealers, and the point is that copies are traceable. This is a legal and
contractual restriction rather than a technical one &mdash; the machine could
cut it, but nobody can lawfully supply the blank.</p>

<p><strong>Transponder car keys.</strong> Since the late 1990s most vehicle
keys carry a chip in the head that the car interrogates before it will start.
Cutting the metal perfectly gets you a key that turns the ignition and does
nothing else. The chip has to be programmed to that specific vehicle, which
needs diagnostic equipment, not a key machine.</p>

<p><strong>Laser-cut and high-security keys.</strong> Sidewinder car keys with a
wavy channel milled into the flat of the blade, and high-security residential
keys with drilled dimples, need different cutting machines than a standard
duplicator.</p>

<h2>Copy from the best original you have</h2>

<p>This is the tip that saves the most trouble. A duplicate is cut from your
key, so any wear on that key is copied onto the new one, and errors compound.
Copy from a copy a few times and eventually you get one that sticks.</p>

<p>If you have an original from the lock &mdash; or the least-used key on the
ring &mdash; bring that one, not the worn one in your pocket. And if a key has
started needing a wiggle to turn, that is the moment to cut fresh copies from
the best original, before the good one is lost.</p>

<h2>What to bring</h2>

<p>Bring the physical key. A photograph or a code number stamped on the lock is
not enough for a standard duplicator, which works by tracing your key's actual
profile. If you are having several cut, say so up front &mdash; they are cut
sequentially and a handful takes a few minutes.</p>

